data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"kind\": \"decision\", \"title\": \"Where should carts live: Redis or Postgres?\", \"body\": \"Carts outgrow the 4 KB session cookie and get truncated. Move cart contents server-side and keep only an opaque cart ID in the cookie.\\n\\n- **Redis:** sub-millisecond reads, TTL-based expiry suits abandoned carts, and the data model is simple. Costs: another service to run and back up, and cart data is lost on eviction or a misconfigured persistence setting unless you configure it.\\n- **Postgres:** carts survive restarts and are queryable for abandoned-cart reports. You likely already run it, so there is no new infra. Costs: slightly slower writes on every cart change, and expiry needs a cleanup job.\\n\\nRecommendation: Postgres, unless you already operate Redis and need very high cart-write volume.\", \"actions\": [{\"label\":\"Use Postgres (Recommended)\",\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"prompt\":\"Store carts in Postgres; keep only a cart ID in the cookie.\"},{\"label\":\"Use Redis\",\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"prompt\":\"Store carts in Redis with TTL expiry; keep only a cart ID in the cookie.\"}]}"}}

